frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Padouk Eb5 over a closed-open tube of Part II's measured A4 radius, its stop set to sit the tube on this bar; four of its elastic ratios padouk's (G_LT/E_L from Bremaud et al. 2010; E_T/E_L, E_R/E_L and G_LR/E_L from Bremaud, Gril and Thibaut 2011, same-genus estimates), its undercut solved again on them, and the broadband observer as its receiver; everything else as the rendered Eb5 card.
name	padouk-eb5-tubetuned-rubber-f4
source	Bar: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544: Bar No. 5 (G4, 397 Hz), xylophone CONCORDE X4001, padouk, L 29.3 cm, b 3.7 cm, h 1.9 cm edge; E, density, gamma_B through the packs named below. Eta 3.86e-7 s is read from Fig. 4 of the same paper (alpha = 6.11 + 7.62e-6 f^2, Eq. 19), 4.22 times Table I's 9.16e-8; why the two differ is not known and they may not be the same bar. Ratios: G_LT/E_L of African padouk heartwood, 1/(a' + 2 nu) = 0.103, with a' = 8.8 from Bremaud et al., Identification of anisotropic vibrational properties of Padauk wood with interlocked grain, Wood Sci. Technol. 44(3), 355-367 (2010), Table 1 and Eq. 2, and nu the paper's "about 0.46", not a measurement. E_T/E_L = 1/11.5, E_R/E_L = 1/7 and G_LR/E_L = 1/9 from the African padauk (Pterocarpus soyauxii) mean row of Bremaud, Gril and Thibaut, Anisotropy of wood vibrational properties: dependence on grain angle and review of literature data, Wood Sci. Technol. 45(4), 735-754 (2011), Table 4, each marked there "Values adjusted by comparing two species of the same genus with similar axial properties": estimates by the group that measured the padauk specimens, not measurements on padauk; the 2010 paper says its 11.5 came from another Pterocarpus species (Caldersmith and Freeman 1990, not read), so where it was measured is not known. G_RT/E_L and the three Poisson ratios are the USDA Wood Handbook FPL-GTR-282's Honduras mahogany, a stand-in species, with no padouk value in them. Tube: V. Doutaut, D. Matignon and A. Chaigne, Numerical simulations of xylophones. II, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 104(3), 1633-1647 (1998): the radius of its A4 resonator, closed at one end, a_T = 1.85 cm (p.1641; measured in Fig. 9, p.1643). Gap: W. E. Flynt, The Construction and Tuning of Vibrating Bars, AMICA Bulletin, December 1972 and March 1973, revised for Mechanical Music Digest, January 2009: "About 1/4 to 1/2 inch spacing is common"; the 1/2 in end.
note	NOT in the source: the tube's stop. No source prints a stop for this tube, so this card sets it as a maker would, at 125.302 mm, solved on the passive column with the bar's reflection in it so its first resonance sits on this bar's own computed f1 of 622.254 Hz, from this card's modes: the stop solved for the cut before these ratios, 125.154 mm, left the tube 1.874 c above this bar's f1 in its render, over the 0.1 c that sets it again, and the law read on that render gave 125.302 mm; rendered there the tube sits +0.007 c from f1, and the law reads 125.303 mm; no recording enters. NOT in the source either: the undercut's shape on this note (solved here in the engine on this card's ratios and 112 x 6 x 5 mesh, a circular arc, reach 54.453 mm, middle 14.480 mm, so f1 sits at equal temperament and f2 = 3 f1; the printed bar itself is tuned about 1:4 at 397 Hz, Table II; the 1:3 target is from xylophone tuning practice in print, not from the recording). The tube's radius is Part II's measured A4 resonator's, 18.5 mm, read as the inside radius and carried to this note, 6 semitones above A4; Part II names no instrument for that tube (its G4 comparisons are on the CONCORDE X4001 of Part I) and prints no wall or stopper. The gap is 12.7 mm below the plane of the uncut lower face, so the wood at the bar's middle is a further 4.5 mm from the mouth: the 1/2 in end of Flynt's printed 1/4 to 1/2 in, the end nearer Part II's "a few centimeters only", chosen before any render. The tube is a closed-open column of three odd modes, every one under this card's band that the open-end law takes (ka under 1.5), coupled through the bar's own boundary elements at each tube mode's frequency; its wall doesn't scatter, the mouth's sound doesn't reflect off the bar on its way out, and there are no neighbouring bars or frame. Its mouth, 37.0 mm across inside, is as wide as this bar. Mallet: the printed rubber row, 1.0 m/s, strike 15.9 cm, Table I and Fig. 8. Iowa's bars are not known to be padouk. This groove isn't known to be converged in mesh: for an earlier cut of this bar, at 112 x 6 x 4 the first partial fell 5.9 c, and this cut was not run at 112 x 6 x 4; it is not known that a maker would cut it.
